now one group of people likely to be  vulnerable during the coming extreme  temperatures are the homeless the mayor  of london sadiq khan says emergency  measures have now been put in place to  help  this year for the first time we've  issued a severe weather emergency  response in summer normally we do this  in winter when it gets really cold and  it's dangerous for people to be sleeping  out in the cold weathers what we've done  this year working with excellent  councils and charities  is issued a severe weather emergency  response now  skies matthew thompson has been out this  morning with volunteers in london  you join me from victoria station which  is in london's city of westminster which  is the london borough with the highest  number of known rough sleepers and by  extension the highest number in the  country it's three times more rough  sleepers here in westminster than there  are in the next highest london borough  which is camden so it's an acute problem  most of the year particularly so though  when the temperatures are set to rise as  high as they are so we've been out with  workers from the charity mungos who are  doing things like providing water  providing sun cream shelter to  vulnerable people who are out on the  streets i caught up with one of the  workers matthew griffiths a short time  ago to explain why it's so important the  charity does what it does  during the hot weather  our client group tend to be more  vulnerable they have various physical  health conditions that make them more  vulnerable to the heat some of them are  on medications such as antipsychotic  medications and epileptic medications  which  don't react well with the heat  and also some of our clients who have  issues with  substance use issues and mental health  tend not to look after themselves when  it gets really hot so you'll find them  in big groups in the daytime hot spots  um  in the blazing sun maybe drinking and  and  not looking after themselves so we want  to encourage them to take respite and  stay hydrated basically  now of course homelessness is just one  area of concern there are many others  like transport for example railways  likely to be affected tarmac apparently  can melt the temperatures that we might  be expecting on monday and tuesday in  large parts of england also the nhs will  struggle increasing numbers of people  presenting with heat exhaustion heat  stroke and things like that ambulances  overstretched we understand there's  going to be a potential cobra meeting  government cobra meeting later today as  they try and coordinate the crisis so  it's fairly clear that across a host of  issues we're going to expect fairly  widespread disruption to british life  come monday and tuesday next week
 

